WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.625
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:03.420
DEBI CABRERA: Data comes
in many shapes and sizes.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.180
In the last videos, we
introduced Apache Beam

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.240
and walked through a
batch data processing job.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.480
But how do you handle
processing streaming data?

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.550
Let's get into it.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.620
First things first, we need
to define batch processing

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.450
and stream processing.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.990
Batch processing uses data
that has a fixed size,

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:33.260
because it represents a data
set that's already completed.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.060
We went through one
example by looking

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.280
at all of the words
in Shakespeare's "King

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.260
Lear" in the last video.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.050
But it could also be
a much larger data

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:46.230
set, like all the words in
every book in a library.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.510
Once you get your hands
on all of the data,

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.930
you can start processing it.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.990
Beam refers to this
type of data as bounded,

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:57.590
because you already have all the
data you'll want to work with.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.380
Stream processing
uses unbounded data,

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.570
where you don't have
a complete set of data

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.849
because it's still
being updated.

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:07.370
If you think about
a social media feed,

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:10.730
for example, there's constantly
new data being added.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.910
And you may need to process
new events as they come in.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:16.520
Since the data set
won't ever be complete,

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.650
you need to process
events as they stream in

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:21.350
and compute results
on aggregated data.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.630
In order to process
unbounded data,

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.730
Beam uses something
called windows.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:28.520
Let's break down
what windows are.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:32.810
In Beam, a window generally
represents a range of time.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:36.050
By default, Beam will
put every piece of data

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:38.450
into the global window,
which represents

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:41.930
the range of time between
negative infinity and infinity.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.500
When you perform any operations
or transforms on your data,

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.180
they will apply to
the entire window.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.450
This is what you'll
usually want when working

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.560
with a batch processing job.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.070
But what happens when you're
working with an unbounded data

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:55.970
set?

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.190
Instead of using the
entire global window,

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.610
you can divide elements between
smaller intervals of time.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.590
These windows
represent time ranges,

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.720
so you can process
the data in each one.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.440
For example, you could
have 60-minute windows

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.840
and count the number of
elements in each window.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.180
The count of each
window would tell you

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:18.350
how many pieces of data were
processed for each hour.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.510
Because each window is
eventually complete,

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.880
you can process them
independently and produce

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:25.880
a final answer for that window.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.400
The global window will
contain all the data

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.930
as it's being processed.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.420
But that's a pretty
large range of time.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.120
When you define how you want
your data to be windowed,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.460
you can configure
things like how large

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.760
the windows are and whether
or not they'll overlap.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.100
Before we dive deeper
into exactly how windows

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.930
work though, the
right configuration

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:47.750
depends on how you want
to process your data based

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.310
on its timestamp.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.800
But what do we mean
by a timestamp?

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.570
Well, if we think
about an event that

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.880
happens on a mobile
device, it's easy enough

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.800
to grab the time that
the event happened,

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.460
according to the device.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.120
We call this event time.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.300
However, that event will also
need to be sent to a system,

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.280
like Beam, for processing.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.680
There will always be a
difference between the time

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.020
that the event actually
happened and when

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.365
the event is processed,
or the processing time.

00:03:16.365 --> 00:03:17.990
Especially when you're
receiving events

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:20.450
from multiple devices with
different connectivity

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.240
and latency, these
events will almost always

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.070
arrive out of order.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:27.950
If you use processing time
to aggregate your events,

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:31.100
you'll only get an
approximate result at best.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.440
Instead, you'll want
to use the event

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.050
time to handle your
data correctly and get

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:37.590
accurate results.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.470
Let's take a look
at an example so we

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:43.070
can see why processing time
won't give you accurate results

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.070
and go over how windows work.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:48.410
Here's a simple example
data set of events

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:51.080
that you might see for
a mobile application.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.030
We can see that
each of these events

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:55.730
is for the same user
ID and each item

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:58.400
represents different user
actions, like tapping

00:03:58.400 --> 00:03:59.780
or swiping.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:02.600
For this data, the
event time property

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.020
will represent the actual time
that the event happened and was

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:07.970
collected on the mobile device.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.080
To keep things simple
for this video,

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.710
we'll look at the event
time as a number of seconds

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:16.230
rather than showing a
full Unix timestamp.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:19.100
If we wanted to aggregate and
count the number of events

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.370
for this user, it would be a
pretty straightforward count.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:24.920
We could group the
events by the user ID

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:26.660
and then count them,
which would give us

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:30.080
a total count of five events.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:32.060
As we mentioned before,
even though we're

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.460
looking at these events
ordered by timestamp,

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.070
there's no guarantee that
they'll actually arrive

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.650
for processing in this order.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:41.600
Data in a distributed
environment

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.370
may be inherently unordered.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.310
And the Apache Beam model
doesn't make any assumptions

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:47.690
about data ordering.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.060
That's why we mentioned
in earlier videos

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:54.330
that peak elections are
immutable and unordered.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:54.830
OK.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:56.480
Back to our example data.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:58.160
We know that there
are some events

00:04:58.160 --> 00:04:59.570
and we want to process them.

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:01.820
If we had all of the
events ahead of time,

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.710
we could consider
it bounded data

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:05.990
and use a batch processing job.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:09.230
In reality, we'll often want
to process unbounded data

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:12.530
with the possibility of more
events coming in at any time

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:14.840
and handle events
arriving out of order.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.870
That means we'll need windows.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.270
If we don't configure
anything for windows,

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:24.080
Beam will place all of our
elements in the global window.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.600
If we wanted to get a
count for the events,

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:28.970
we'd have to wait for
the data to be complete.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:32.150
And since our example
represents unbounded data,

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:34.130
new events could
arrive at any time

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.810
and the window will
never be complete.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:39.530
There is a way to get
speculative results when

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:42.080
working with unbounded
data, but we'll talk more

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:43.910
about that in another video.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:46.760
In order to get the complete
results we're looking for,

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.190
we'll need to subdivide
the data into groups

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:53.240
that we know will eventually
complete using windows.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.760
We'll start by
using fixed windows.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.970
These are the windows of the
same size that repeat, like,

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:01.760
every hour or every 15 seconds.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.830
Using our same data
from before, we

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:07.460
can use fixed windows that
have a size of five seconds.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:10.220
Using these windows, we
can do a simple aggregation

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:12.920
by counting the data
inside of each window

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:15.330
after the window is complete.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:18.620
So the first window has
a count of three events

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:21.530
and the second window has
a count of two events.

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:23.630
The right size for
your window depends

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.880
on your use case and your data.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.100
But you can specify
the length to Beam

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:30.530
and it takes care of the rest.

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:33.990
Beam can estimate and track
when a window is complete,

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:37.530
which we'll talk about
more later in this video.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.610
However, we did talk about
this being unbounded data.

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:43.280
That means more events
could come in at any time.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:45.770
And they're not guaranteed
to arrive in the same order

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:47.330
that they happened.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:51.110
And just like that,
here is a new event.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:53.990
While this event looks the
same as our other ones,

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:57.000
the event time is
actually at 4 seconds.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.830
So the event itself would've
happened before the events that

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:03.410
happened at 6 and 8 seconds.

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:06.680
Since data like this can
continue to arrive at any time,

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:09.770
Beam needs to determine when
to stop adding new events

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:14.360
and process the data inside
of a window to produce output.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:17.390
Triggers are the mechanisms
that Beam actually

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:21.200
uses to emit the aggregated
results for each window.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:25.250
The default afterwatermark
trigger can be overridden.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:27.320
And there's multiple
types of triggers

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:29.240
that you can customize,
depending on how

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.050
you want to process your data.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.550
We'll talk more about those
in the next video too.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.130
When collecting and
grouping data into windows,

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:41.190
Beam uses a watermark to
keep track of how up to date

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.850
the events in that window are.

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:45.800
As data flows
through a pipeline,

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.890
Beam keeps track of the
oldest event timestamp that it

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:51.080
expects to see at each step.

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.450
This is how Beam can
handle and estimate

00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:55.220
when a window is
actually complete

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:58.100
and all the data is
expected to have arrived,

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:02.060
which also means Beam can
process the complete window.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.400
Once the watermark for
a window has passed,

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.740
the default
afterwatermark trigger

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.500
will emit the results
for that window.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:12.770
Any data that arrives for
that window after this point

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:16.130
is considered late
data, which we'll also

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.240
discuss in the next video.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.020
Let's see what this looks
like with our example data.

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:23.720
Since we're trying to illustrate
the differences between event

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:26.150
time and processing
time, we'll take a look

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:29.760
at when these events
actually arrived in Beam.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.120
This is a pretty
optimistic example,

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.610
because most of our event
time and processing times

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:36.900
are very close together.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:39.140
However, the last event
has a bit more delay.

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:41.210
Since we're using
5-second windows,

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:44.360
the data will be split
into two windows.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:47.510
The tap event at 8
seconds was actually

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:49.490
processed at 9 seconds.

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:53.150
But the overall data would still
be processed in the same way,

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:56.360
since that event is still
part of the same window.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:59.330
On the other hand,
the final swipe event

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:04.190
has an event time of 4 seconds
but was processed at 9 seconds.

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:07.040
If we were using the
processing time in our windows,

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.350
we'd have three
events in each window.

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:12.710
But that doesn't reflect when
the events actually happened.

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:15.350
We would normally want
that new swipe event

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:18.440
in the first window, since
that's actually accurate as

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:20.540
to when the event happened.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:22.790
So we can see that
using event time

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.470
is the right way to
get accurate results.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:27.380
But Beam still
needs a way to know

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:31.010
when it can expect all the data
in each window to have arrived.

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:34.070
Since out of order data
could arrive at any time,

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:37.310
Beam needs to use a watermark
to estimate when the window is

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:38.690
actually complete.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:40.730
Let's look at just the
events that would be

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:42.800
contained by our first window.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.110
Because the process
time for the final event

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.540
is after the closing
time for the window,

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:49.550
whether or not it makes
it into that window

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:51.470
depends on the watermark.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:54.620
The delay in reordering
between event arrival

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:56.040
can vary over time.

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:59.600
So the watermark will make
progress at different rates.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.460
If the watermark for
this window hasn't

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:04.280
passed until after the
new event is processed,

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:06.950
then that event will
make it into the window.

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:09.170
Once the watermark
passes, the window

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:13.230
will be processed with
all four events in it.

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:16.640
But if Beam determines that
the watermark has passed

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:18.800
before the new
event is processed,

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:22.090
Beam would close the window
without including the event

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:24.880
and discard it as late data.

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.880
This is the default behavior
for handling late data.

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:30.620
And just like most of what we
talked about in this video,

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:33.460
you can customize how
late data is handled.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.280
So to recap what we've
talked about in this video,

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:38.960
there's bounded and
unbounded data sets,

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:41.920
which are aligned with
batch and stream processing.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:44.320
Beam uses windows
to handle data,

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.280
dividing up the
data into segments

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:48.010
so it's easier to work with.

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:51.250
Fixed windows have distinct
starting and ending

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:53.890
times for the data
that goes into them.

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.560
Using event time gives
you accurate results

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:58.810
rather than using
processing time.

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:01.690
And Beam uses afterwatermark
as the default

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:04.060
trigger to know when
there is no more data

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:06.130
to be expected in a window.

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:08.890
We'll talk about handling
late data, triggers,

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:11.360
and different kinds of
windows in the next video.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:14.760
So stay tuned and
thanks for watching.

